import{at as e,it as t,n,vt as r}from"./chunks/framework.UwwvkjGo.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Console TUI ballot","description":"","frontmatter":{"head":[["meta",{"property":"og:title","content":"Console TUI ballot"}],["meta",{"property":"og:description","content":""}],["meta",{"property":"og:url","content":"https://jsprismarine.github.io/JSPrismarine/research/console-tui/ballot"}],["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://jsprismarine.github.io/JSPrismarine/research/console-tui/ballot"}]],"editSource":{"kind":"file","url":"https://github.com/JSPrismarine/JSPrismarine/edit/master/docs/research/console-tui/ballot.md"}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"research/console-tui/ballot.md","filePath":"research/console-tui/ballot.md","lastUpdated":1794040891000}`),a={name:`research/console-tui/ballot.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return r(),t(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[e(`<div><h1 id="console-tui-ballot" tabindex="-1">Console TUI ballot <a class="header-anchor" href="#console-tui-ballot" aria-label="Permalink to “Console TUI ballot”">​</a></h1><p>Candidate designs for moving the JSPrismarine console out of the server process and rebuilding it on OpenTUI. Judges read the four research notes in this directory (<code>opentui.md</code>, <code>process-separation.md</code>, <code>ux-design.md</code>, <code>repo-map.md</code>) and vote on each question below.</p><h2 id="question-1-topology-and-framework" tabindex="-1">Question 1: topology and framework <a class="header-anchor" href="#question-1-topology-and-framework" aria-label="Permalink to “Question 1: topology and framework”">​</a></h2><h3 id="option-a-host-first-plain-opentui-core" tabindex="-1">Option A: host-first, plain <code>@opentui/core</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#option-a-host-first-plain-opentui-core" aria-label="Permalink to “Option A: host-first, plain @opentui/core”">​</a></h3><ul><li>One entry point. With a TTY on stdout (and Node &gt;= 26.4), <code>prismarine</code> becomes the host: it spawns <code>process.execPath</code> with the same script plus a hidden <code>--role=server</code> argument (or env var), <code>stdio: [&#39;ignore&#39;, &#39;pipe&#39;, &#39;pipe&#39;, &#39;ipc&#39;]</code>, runs the OpenTUI renderer, and alone owns raw mode. Without a TTY, with <code>--no-tui</code>, or on Node &lt; 26.4, it runs the server in-process and writes plain lines to stdout as today.</li><li>The server child is constructed <code>headless: true</code>, adds an IPC-backed console transport with a bounded ring buffer and <code>log.dropped</code>, runs <code>command</code> messages through <code>dispatchCommand</code>, and shuts down on <code>shutdown</code> or IPC <code>disconnect</code>.</li><li><code>packages/server/scripts/dev.mts</code> watchers and bedrock-data regeneration move into the host behind <code>--watch</code>; supervisor events render as a status segment and toasts.</li><li>TUI built from plain <code>@opentui/core</code> renderables, organized as components (classes or factory functions) with a small store, no React or Solid, no JSX toolchain.</li><li>Protocol: versioned discriminated unions, JSON-only bodies, stream adapter interface so an NDJSON socket adapter (attach mode) can come later.</li></ul><h3 id="option-b-server-first-attachable-socket" tabindex="-1">Option B: server-first, attachable socket <a class="header-anchor" href="#option-b-server-first-attachable-socket" aria-label="Permalink to “Option B: server-first, attachable socket”">​</a></h3><ul><li>The server stays the main process and always listens on a local Unix socket or Windows named pipe inside <code>JSP_DIR</code>. <code>prismarine console</code> (or <code>prismarine attach</code>) connects with the OpenTUI client. Multiple consoles may attach; the server replays the last N records on connect.</li><li>Dev flow keeps <code>dev.mts</code> as a separate supervisor; it spawns the server and must also forward its own notices to the attached console (a second socket or a message relayed via the server).</li><li>Plain <code>@opentui/core</code>.</li></ul><h3 id="option-c-host-first-opentui-react" tabindex="-1">Option C: host-first, <code>@opentui/react</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#option-c-host-first-opentui-react" aria-label="Permalink to “Option C: host-first, @opentui/react”">​</a></h3><ul><li>Same process topology and protocol as Option A, but the TUI is written as React function components with JSX, using <code>@opentui/react</code> and <code>react</code>.</li></ul><h2 id="question-2-where-the-protocol-and-transport-code-lives" tabindex="-1">Question 2: where the protocol and transport code lives <a class="header-anchor" href="#question-2-where-the-protocol-and-transport-code-lives" aria-label="Permalink to “Question 2: where the protocol and transport code lives”">​</a></h2><ul><li>2a. A new package <code>@jsprismarine/console-protocol</code> (types, codec, adapters) depended on by both <code>@jsprismarine/prismarine</code> (server side transport) and the TUI package.</li><li>2b. Inside the TUI package <code>@jsprismarine/console</code> as a subpath export, with <code>prismarine</code> depending on <code>@jsprismarine/console/protocol</code> (which would pull OpenTUI into the server&#39;s dependency tree).</li><li>2c. Inside <code>@jsprismarine/logger</code> next to <code>LogRecord</code>.</li></ul><h2 id="question-3-log-pane-rendering" tabindex="-1">Question 3: log pane rendering <a class="header-anchor" href="#question-3-log-pane-rendering" aria-label="Permalink to “Question 3: log pane rendering”">​</a></h2><ul><li>3a. One <code>TextRenderable</code> per record inside a <code>ScrollBoxRenderable</code> with sticky scroll (simplest, measured 6 to 50 ms per update beyond 5 000 lines, 65 536 native object cap).</li><li>3b. Records in a JS ring buffer; a custom <code>LogView</code> component renders only the visible window into a bounded set of renderables (measured 0.2 to 0.5 ms per update), with its own scroll offset, follow mode, and new-lines badge.</li></ul><h2 id="question-4-fate-of-the-readline-console" tabindex="-1">Question 4: fate of the readline console <a class="header-anchor" href="#question-4-fate-of-the-readline-console" aria-label="Permalink to “Question 4: fate of the readline console”">​</a></h2><ul><li>4a. Keep <code>packages/prismarine/src/Console.ts</code> as the in-process fallback (non-TTY, <code>--no-tui</code>, Node &lt; 26.4) but strip its raw-mode handling to a plain line reader.</li><li>4b. Delete it; the in-process fallback is output-only (plain stdout) with no interactive input.</li><li>4c. Keep it unchanged as the fallback.</li></ul><h2 id="question-5-ctrl-c" tabindex="-1">Question 5: Ctrl+C <a class="header-anchor" href="#question-5-ctrl-c" aria-label="Permalink to “Question 5: Ctrl+C”">​</a></h2><ul><li>5a. First press clears the input line; second press on an empty line stops the server, with a hint in the status bar.</li><li>5b. Single press stops the server, as today.</li></ul><h2 id="judging-criteria" tabindex="-1">Judging criteria <a class="header-anchor" href="#judging-criteria" aria-label="Permalink to “Judging criteria”">​</a></h2><p>Weigh, in this order: meets the responsiveness goal under server overload; works headless in containers without new moving parts; fits the SEA packaging path; modularity and ease of extending the TUI; implementation effort and risk given the repository&#39;s constraints (Node floor 22.18, Biome, JSDoc check, no JSX config, Node 26 in CI); prior art.</p></div>`,1)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};